        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹəʊhɪŋ.d͡ʒə/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹəʊhɪŋ.jə/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>pn</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>rohinyá</ns0:quote>
          <ns0:quote>ruaingá</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>The Indo-European language spoken in Arakan, Myanmar, closely related to Chittagonian.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Rolle's theorem</ns0:orth>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>pn</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>teorema de Rolle</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>(calculus) The theorem that any real-valued differentiable function that attains equal values at two distinct points must have a point somewhere between them where the first derivative (the slope of the tangent line to the graph of the function) is zero. In mathematical terms, if f : \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R} is differentiable on (a,b) and f(a)=f(b) then \exists c \in (a,b) : f'(c)=0.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Rom</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹoʊm/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹəʊm/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>n</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>gitana</ns0:quote>
          <ns0:quote>gitano</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>A member of the Romani people.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>(in particular) A male member of the Romani people who is married and considered respectable amongst the family.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Roma</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹoʊmə/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹəʊmə/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>pn</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>Romaní</ns0:quote>
          <ns0:quote>Romaníes</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>A variety of the Romani language (or occasionally) the Romani macrolanguage.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romagna</ns0:orth>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>pn</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>Romaña</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>A historic region in northern Italy that formed part of modern Emilia-Romagna.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Roman</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹoʊ.mən/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹəʊ.mən/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>adj</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>romano</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>Of or from Rome.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>(historical) Of or from the Roman Empire.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Roman</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹoʊ.mən/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹəʊ.mən/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>n</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>romano</ns0:quote>
          <ns0:quote>romana</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>A native or resident of Rome.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Roman</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹoʊ.mən/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹəʊ.mən/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>pn</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>Román</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>A en given name recently borrowed from continental Europe.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Roman Catholic Church</ns0:orth>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>pn</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>Iglesia católica romana</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>Latin Church, the largest of the autonomous particular churches of the Catholic Church.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Roman Empire</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹoʊmən ˈɛmpaɪɚ/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹəʊmən ˈɛmpaɪə/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>pn</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>Imperio romano</ns0:quote>
          <ns0:quote>Imperio Romano</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>(historical) An ancient empire based out of Rome, which succeeded the Roman Republic and existed between 27 B.C.E. and 476 C.E. in the west (and until 1453 C.E. in the east; see Byzantine Empire), encompassing vast territories in Europe, Asia and Africa, stretching from Britain and Germany to Spain, North Africa and the Persian Gulf. [from 15th c.]</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Roman numeral</ns0:orth>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>n</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>número romano</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>A numeral represented using a Roman system involving the letters I, V, X, L, C, D, and M.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Roman numerals</ns0:orth>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>n</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>números romanos</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>(uncountable) The system of numerals using Roman numerals.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Roman road</ns0:orth>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>n</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>calzada romana</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>(historical) Any of many roads built in Europe, including Britain, by the Ancient Romans, many of which survive in various forms.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romance</ns0:orth>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>adj</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>romance</ns0:quote>
          <ns0:quote>románico</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>Of or dealing with languages or cultures derived from Roman influence and Latin: French, Italian, Portuguese, Spanish, Galician, Romanian, Catalan, Occitan, etc.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romance</ns0:orth>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>n</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>idiomas románicos / romances</ns0:quote>
          <ns0:quote>lenguas románicas / romances</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>The group of languages and cultures which are derived from Vulgar Latin. [from 17th c.]</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romance language</ns0:orth>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>n</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>lengua romance</ns0:quote>
          <ns0:quote>lengua románica</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>Any language descended from Latin.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romandy</ns0:orth>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>pn</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>Romandía</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>The French-speaking part of Switzerland.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romanesque</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ˌɹoʊməˈnɛsk/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ˌɹəʊməˈnɛsk/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>adj</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>románico</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>Somewhat resembling the Romans; applied sometimes to the debased style of the later Roman Empire, but especially to the more developed art and architecture prevailing from the 8th century to the 12th.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romani</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹoʊ.mə.ni/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹɑ.mə.ni/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹɒ.mə.ni/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹɒm.ni/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹəʊ.mə.ni/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>pn</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>romaní</ns0:quote>
          <ns0:quote>caló</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>The Indo-Aryan lect of the Roma people, or one of its sublects (such as Roma, Sinti, Romanichal, etc), closely related to Hindi and Rajasthani.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romania</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹoʊˈmeɪ.ni.ə/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹoˈmaː.nja/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹuˈmeɪ.ni.ə/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹəʊˈmeɪ.ni.ə/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹʊˈmeɪ.ni.ə/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>pn</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>Rumania</ns0:quote>
          <ns0:quote>Rumanía</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>A country in Southeast Europe. Capital and largest city: Bucharest.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romanian</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹoʊˈmeɪ.ni.ən/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹoˈmɪ.ni.ən/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹəʊˈmeɪ.ni.ən/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹəˈmeɪ.ni.ən/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>adj</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>rumano</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>Of or relating to Romania, the Romanian people, or the Romanian language.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romanian</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹoʊˈmeɪ.ni.ən/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹoˈmɪ.ni.ən/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹəʊˈmeɪ.ni.ən/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹəˈmeɪ.ni.ən/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>n</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>rumano</ns0:quote>
          <ns0:quote>rumana</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>(countable) A native of Romania.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>rumano</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>(uncountable) The official language of Romania and Moldova.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romanic</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹoʊˈmæn.ɪk/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹəʊˈmæn.ɪk/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>adj</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>románico</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>Of or relating to Rome or its people, especially Ancient Rome.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romanization</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ˌɹəʊmənaɪˈzeɪʃən/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ˌɹəʊmənɪˈzeɪʃən/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>n</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>romanización</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>(usually, uncountable) The act or process of putting text into the Latin (Roman) alphabet, by means such as transliteration and transcription.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romanoff</ns0:orth>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>pn</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>Románov</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>A dynasty that ruled the Russian Empire</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>An aristocratic house that formed a ruling dynasty of Russia</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romans</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹoʊmənz/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹəʊmənz/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>pn</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>Romanos</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>(biblical) The sixth book of the New Testament of the Bible, the epistle of St Paul to the Christians in Rome.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>The 30th sura (chapter) of the Qur'an.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romansch</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹoʊˈmænʃ/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹoʊˈmɑnʃ/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹə(ʊ)ˈmanʃ/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>pn</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>romanche</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>(strict sense) A Rhaeto-Romance language (or dialect continuum) spoken in the Graubünden/Grisons canton of Switzerland; it is one of the four official languages of Switzerland.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romantic</ns0:orth>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>adj</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>romántico</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>Of or pertaining to Romanticism.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romanticism</ns0:orth>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>pn</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>Romanticismo</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>(arts, music) An artistic, literary, musical, and intellectual movement that originated in Europe towards the end of the 18th century, peaking from 1800 to 1850, characterized by its emphasis on emotion and individualism as well as glorification of all the past and nature, preferring the medieval rather than the classical.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Rome</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹoʊm/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹum/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ɹəʊm/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>pn</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>Roma</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>A major city, the capital of Italy and the Italian region of Lazio, located on the Tiber River; the ancient capital of the Roman Empire.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>(historical) An ancient empire based out of the city of Rome, covering vast territories in Europe, Asia and Africa; in full, Roman Empire.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Rome wasn't built in a day</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹoʊm ˌwʌzənt ˈbɪlt‿ɪn‿ə ˈdeɪ/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹəʊm ˌwɒzənt ˈbɪlt‿ɪn‿ə ˈdeɪ/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>proverb</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>no se ganó Zamora en una hora</ns0:quote>
          <ns0:quote>Roma no se hizo en un día</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>(idiomatic) It takes a long time to create something complicated or impressive.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romeo</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ˈɹoʊmioʊ/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>pn</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>Romeo</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>(fiction) One of the main characters of Romeo and Juliet by William Shakespeare: the ardent lover of Juliet.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Romeo and Juliet</ns0:orth>
      </ns0:form>
