      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>yidis</ns0:quote>
          <ns0:quote>yídish</ns0:quote>
          <ns0:quote>ídish</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>A West Germanic, or more specifically High German, language that developed from Middle High German dialects, with an admixture of vocabulary from multiple source languages including Hebrew-Aramaic, Romance, Slavic, English, etc., and mostly written in Hebrew characters which is used mainly among Ashkenazic Jews from central and eastern Europe.</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
    <ns0:entry xmlns:ns0="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
      <ns0:form>
        <ns0:orth>Yoda condition</ns0:orth>
        <ns0:pron>/ˌjoʊdə kənˈdɪʃən/</ns0:pron>
        <ns0:pron>/ˌjəʊdə kənˈdɪʃn̩/</ns0:pron>
      </ns0:form>
      <ns0:gramGrp>
        <ns0:pos>n</ns0:pos>
      </ns0:gramGrp>
      <ns0:sense>
        <ns0:cit type="trans" xml:lang="es">
          <ns0:quote>condición Yoda</ns0:quote>
        </ns0:cit>
        <ns0:sense>
          <ns0:def>(programming, informal) A logical condition with the usual order of operands reversed for various reasons, such as avoiding accidental misuse of = (assignment) instead of == (equality), a mistake that is harder to spot when using the normal order of operands. [from early 21st c.]</ns0:def>
        </ns0:sense>
      </ns0:sense>
    </ns0:entry>
